.TH PSTATE 8
.SH NAME
pstate \- the processors' clock (P-states)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I (in plan9.ini)
.B pstate=
.RB [ auto | high | low | off ]
.PP
.B aux/pstate
[
.B -dn
]
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
]
[
.B -s
.I service
]
[
.BR auto | high | low
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Aux/pstate
sets the clock of the machine's processors through their
model-specific registers
.RB ( #P/msr ,
see
.IR arch (3)).
It posts
.BI /srv/ service
(default
.BR pstate )
and mounts it after
.I mountpoint
(default
.BR /dev ),
where it serves one file,
.BR pstate .
.I Termrc
and
.I cpurc
start it only when
.IR plan9.ini (8)
sets
.B pstate=
to
.BR auto ,
.B high
or
.BR low ,
the first policy; without it (the default for now) the clock stays as
the firmware set it.
.PP
The method follows the processor:
.TF intel-eist
.TP
.B intel-hwp
Intel's hardware P-states (Skylake and newer): the processor picks
its clock between a minimum and a maximum, after an
energy/performance preference.
.TP
.B amd-cppc
AMD's CPPC through its MSRs (Zen 2 and newer, where the firmware
offers it): the same.
.TP
.B intel-eist
Enhanced SpeedStep by ratio (Nehalem to Broadwell), with turbo.
.TP
.B amd-pstate
AMD's P-state registers (family 10h and newer, also where the
firmware's CPPC is described by ACPI only); boost comes by itself in
the fastest state.
.PD
.PP
Switching on HWP or CPPC holds until the next reset; the
processor then manages its clock alone.
For the other two
.I aux/pstate
is the governor: every 100 ms it measures each processor's load
(its MPERF counter stops while it halts); it goes to the highest
clock when one processor was busy more than two thirds of the
period or all of them more than half, and to the lowest after half a
second without (OpenBSD's rule).
.PP
Reading
.B pstate
gives lines:
.TF limits
.TP
.BI method " name"
as above, or
.B none
with a line
.BI why " reason"
(a virtual machine, an older processor, SpeedStep off in the
firmware).
.TP
.BI policy " p"
the policy.
.TP
.BI tsc " mhz"
the time stamp counter's rate, the nominal clock.
.TP
.BI cpu N " limits " "lowest nominal highest" " ..."
per processor: its limits in the method's units (EIST: ratios of the
100 MHz bus clock; HWP and CPPC: performance levels; AMD's P-states:
state numbers, 0 the fastest), for the governed methods the
.BR level ,
.B high
or
.BR low ,
and, where the processor counts APERF and MPERF, its clock while not
halted
.RB ( mhz )
and the percentage of the last period it was busy
.RB ( busy ).
.PD
.PP
Writing a policy sets it:
.TF auto
.TP
.B auto
the governor, or for HWP and CPPC the whole range with a balanced
preference.
.TP
.B high
always the highest clock (turbo, boost).
.TP
.B low
always the lowest.
.PD
.PP
Option
.B -d
prints the governor's changes;
.B -n
only prints the method and the first processor's limits and exits,
without switching HWP or CPPC on.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
% cat /dev/pstate
method intel-eist
policy auto
tsc 2594
cpu0 limits 5 26 32 level low mhz 790 busy 2
\&...
% echo high >/dev/pstate
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/pstate.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR arch (3),
.IR acpi (8),
.IR plan9.ini (8)
.SH BUGS
Processors older than Nehalem (Core 2, Pentium M) and Silvermont or
Airmont Atoms would need ACPI's
.B _PSS
tables, which
.I aux/pstate
does not read; it says so and exits.
AMD's CPPC described by ACPI alone
.RB ( _CPC )
is not used: such processors run on their P-state registers.
.PP
9space: not in 9front.
.PP
The probe reads the processor's identity with CPUID, by gcc's inline
assembly. Built by 9front's own compilers, which have none,
.I pstate
finds no P-states and says
.BR "no CPUID" .
